blocks.body:
The logic is always the same. If $\Phi$ is an invariant and $\Phi(\text{start}) \ne \Phi(\text{target})$, the target cannot be reached, however the moves are chosen. The hard part is to find a $\Phi$ that separates the two states. Some quantities worth trying first:

- parity, or more generally a residue modulo some $m$;
- a sum, a product, a sum of squares, or a sum of reciprocals of the numbers present;
- the gcd or lcm of the numbers present;
- a colouring of a board, with $\Phi$ counting cells of each colour.

A classic colouring argument: remove two opposite corners from an $8 \times 8$ chessboard. Can the remaining $62$ cells be covered by $31$ dominoes? Opposite corners have the same colour, so $32$ cells of one colour and $30$ of the other remain. Every domino covers one cell of each colour, so as dominoes are placed one by one, the number of covered cells of the first colour minus the number of the second stays $0$. A full covering would make this difference $32 - 30 = 2$. So no covering exists.

Be clear about what this proves. An invariant can only say no. If start and target have the same value of $\Phi$, that tells you nothing; the target may still be unreachable for some other reason.
